143ce349dc9fecca67a04f025b89d015a8cc.svg) we obtain\n![](images/d2/d2d5dd3f69b5f511e48cad601f49698f8c6f92aa2c98196b61cc3bdcb9e7419f.svg)\nComputing the determinant ![](images/f5/f5c755c47f2a30ae0db319eaf4b5cff6e7280eaade51fef46f4c20aa956d1f6f.svg) we get\n![](images/ee/eee7490a165a97b1ba1a58445cf275a84053d49790e0207cc2c184f74f1b6d3e.svg)\nNow, since ![](images/01/01e2eff1b6607d38b802f0ecfbc86c7a1d7254094270984c88127af751354c97.svg) and  ![](images/9e/9ecaac33ed65181d517c6a3700f9d07067e4e78b8302a4374697432057e47852.svg) the function ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg) has a local minimum at ![](images/ab/ab6b2f25fb90ef8bea8b21d9d0253752c37bb3c77b675547a223b03f97d3bf6a.svg)\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at ![](images/41/41378cd8f4c141c0db7051b606fc860a0c333837e78305a593ce2f5fbd303a32.svg) we obtain\n![](images/84/84190825d65c6d799de0f606c5d0614692d9ec22ba1dc6916a2e6ce6989760d9.svg)\nComputing the determinant ![](images/72/7265b7327f1bfe1fbfb02e958ff01a35790adbc5e1f0984618c97e85e812fca6.svg) we get\n![](images/f0/f0f000997b9e965a8280f51619abfc3c9f6574fbaa6292f045c7d03720693bd9.svg)\nTherefore, ![](images/52/52c679e6f9a0ed40ecf5652d769a673a1cf842a1816d913ddc4e6a3356dc43af.svg) is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, ![](images/a2/a2dcac06a4cf671df0fa43cef1727449fb2ebc57fb824d19cc07da5e75d12176.svg) has a local minimum at ![](images/dc/dcc19bac01833ff23365244490e52b2722741da56e804e9a95b5b264f23216bf.svg) and a saddle point at ![](images/89/897570ce172a9f169127c297c355bc7a446b1df6ee78afe238f5b53dde75a85c.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding and Classifying All Critical Points Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39640287-9487-5ac3-85d7-aedbf5d97773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-4,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434956} {:answer/id #uuid "3c018145-70ed-5572-bd19-1c77a5f9feed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-4,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434957} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c03577-b7e1-599b-a3bf-f0b6d8f8b9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(-4,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434965} {:answer/id #uuid "c5596b05-f02f-56da-a832-f2b3413b0aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434967} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb56293-94a9-5eda-9354-4caedfa03d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39640287-9487-5ac3-85d7-aedbf5d97773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-4,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434956} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf6f62c2-36fa-58b4-83fb-e251b870cb3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "05076731-f2b5-5426-b032-1f0962af8ee1" :question/math-academy-id "q-105278" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=xy-4x-4y-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-4x-4y-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-4x-4y-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}y-4-2x & x-4-2y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y-4-2x=0 \\\\ x-4-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny-4-2x=0\\,⟹\\,y=2x+4\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4-2(2x+4) & =0 \\\\ -3x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=2x+4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=-4\\,⟹\\,y=2(-4)+4=-4.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-4,-4)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-4-2x)=-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-4-2y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-4-2)=1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-4-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-4,-4),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-4,-4)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-4,-4),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-4,-4)=det({f}^{″}(-4,-4))=(-2)^{2}-{1}^{2}=3>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-4,-4)=({f}^{″}(-4,-4))_{11}=-2<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-4,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "045854fc-9cad-5005-a306-9550848e25df" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e2ce651-2667-55e1-9780-7a34d1104ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(6,18)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434953} {:answer/id #uuid "37a0de16-9bbf-5790-940a-6df5f38a572f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,18)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434955} {:answer/id #uuid "57ac445e-e9c7-520d-b29c-4fe87458e1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(18,6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434959} {:answer/id #uuid "a794c07b-fa43-5580-8491-f1a57c1a709a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434962} {:answer/id #uuid "d74a0d2a-151b-543e-af9b-154d12712718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(3,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57ac445e-e9c7-520d-b29c-4fe87458e1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(18,6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434959} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c63413f-ae27-5733-bba1-59d997916a9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5268fbe7-eec3-599a-9bb3-09780fd2d7a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-36635" :question/problem "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x-6y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-6x+3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-6y & -6x+3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-6y=0 \\\\ -6x+3{y}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x-6y=0\\,⟹\\,x=3y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6(3y)+3{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ -18y+3{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3y(-6+y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $x=3y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0 & \\,⟹\\,x=3(0)=0 \\\\ y=6 & \\,⟹\\,x=3(6)=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(0,0)$ and $(18,6).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(0,0)$ and $(18,6).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdb2d3f5-d74a-5708-9193-da559a93e3a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123003} {:answer/id #uuid "f7242f4d-37c6-5a92-aea4-e1451ea3ff87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123004} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd2ad80-e3b2-5853-a503-997a3f10abbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123005} {:answer/id #uuid "3c98691f-2445-5880-944f-87cf5f407b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123006} {:answer/id #uuid "5bda5d0c-e9fa-54b7-8032-3675a6282c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bda5d0c-e9fa-54b7-8032-3675a6282c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123007} :answer-field/id #uuid "37fa1294-0d25-5a87-933a-a3795c1356c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a07adee-cd5f-5906-86f1-fee57d6ba6a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37378" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{x} & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(1,0)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,0)>0$\n\nIII. The point $(1,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=det({f}^{″}(1,0))=2\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,0)=({f}^{″}(1,0))_{11}=2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,0)>0,$ the point $(1,0)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "331ce6e2-6cff-5def-8b6a-3be45abe7c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434954} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca32ab4-8f3a-515a-8ecd-12be5c208e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434958} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7a8c33-3716-54b0-98b7-5f697c4dec91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434960} {:answer/id #uuid "9d123cd6-85f0-5cbf-a5e0-0b316d24c209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434961} {:answer/id #uuid "cec2209f-098a-5ca0-8805-a355b82c4ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cec2209f-098a-5ca0-8805-a355b82c4ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434968} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3de3699-ca1f-5183-a0b0-3f92f5e0689d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b007310c-a11f-531c-b695-1febe8ef0462" :question/math-academy-id "q-37589" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(-1,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(-1,0)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0$\n\nIII. The point $(-1,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-1,0),$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(-1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,0)=det({f}^{″}(-1,0))=8\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=16>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)=({f}^{″}(-1,0))_{11}=8>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(-1,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0,$ the point $(-1,0)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "045854fc-9cad-5005-a306-9550848e25df" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2a4e53-1b67-5b4b-b1ff-24dbbe84d43d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434952} {:answer/id #uuid "ac84a034-8408-51a1-b95f-a12af62e4ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434963} {:answer/id #uuid "af3044d7-995b-5b31-911a-1432e5fb9524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434964} {:answer/id #uuid "ef89c4a6-451f-5b35-895b-c5f4bf15a46a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434973} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c1486d-d897-5524-a326-f95ed8d30db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a2a4e53-1b67-5b4b-b1ff-24dbbe84d43d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434952} :answer-field/id #uuid "4639d8d2-4eac-541e-8594-82bea3d94f3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e309db36-c1df-54f7-9f6d-2cd23acc2d7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80605" :question/problem "Determine the stationary points of $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+xy+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $D=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}∣y>0\\},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x+\\frac{2}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+y & x+\\frac{2}{y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=0 \\\\ x+\\frac{2}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y=0\\,⟹\\,y=-2x.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{2}{-2x} & =0 \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{x} & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=-2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-1 & \\,⟹\\,y=-2(-1)=2 \\\\ x=1 & \\,⟹\\,y=-2(1)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(-1,2)$ and $(1,-2).$ We reject the point $(1,-2)$ because it does not lie in the domain of $f(x,y).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(-1,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124655 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding and Classifying All Critical Points Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c27e9b4e-206f-5150-a881-d37dde9c4e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(3,1)$ and a local maximum $(-3,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434966} {:answer/id #uuid "d2284ea7-6bfd-52fc-b08e-cbc5dc03d189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-3,1)$ and a local minimum $(-3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434969} {:answer/id #uuid "e02c6dc0-e019-5937-b18e-930a68cb954f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(3,1)$ and a local maximum $(-3,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434971} {:answer/id #uuid "e957c6c0-0532-521e-b2c3-2747f7ac1033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-3,1)$ and a saddle point at $(-3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434972} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d5c20b-2412-5e45-a4e7-034876616c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(3,1)$ and $(-3,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5d5c20b-2412-5e45-a4e7-034876616c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(3,1)$ and $(-3,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434975} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce8b2aa5-8481-5045-bb5b-59aa8a2d0aa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a336a18-c506-59a2-baef-25499733a8d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-81179" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2(xy-3) & {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2(xy-3) & {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2(xy-3)\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}xy-3=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nxy-3=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9{(\\frac{3}{x})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{81}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}-81}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-81 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{3}{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=-3\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{-3}=-1,\\,\\,x=3\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-3,-1)$ and $(3,1)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-6)=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-6)=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})=-18y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x \\\\ 2x & -18y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(3,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3,1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(3,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(3,1)=det({f}^{″}(3,1))=2\\cdot (-18)-{6}^{2}=-72<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(3,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-3,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-3,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-3,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-3,-1)=det({f}^{″}(-3,-1))=(-2)\\cdot 18-(-6)^{2}=-72<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(-3,-1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(3,1)$ and $(-3,-1).$"}]}
